1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,480
So we've just done mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:08,880
And now we're going to move on to the concept

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:11,880
of sub-gain perfection.

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,320
This is another one on the short list of abbreviations

5
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:17,280
you need to know.

6
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:22,360
SGPNE, sub-gain perfect Nash equilibrium.

7
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:28,760
And then, of course, there is SGPPSNE,

8
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:31,280
sub-gain perfect pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

9
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:37,800
And SGPMSNE, sub-gain perfect mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.

10
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,560
So these are all in the same family,

11
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:46,240
so we don't really have to analyze three different things.

12
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:50,080
So here is the key idea of sub-gain perfection,

13
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:53,800
which is an additional refinement that is often

14
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:56,640
added on top of Nash equilibrium.

15
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:57,560
And it's like this.

16
00:00:57,560 --> 00:00:59,120
Now, this is just an example.

17
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,120
I'm not actually trying to threaten or scare anyone.

18
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:06,440
But imagine a world where I threaten

19
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:09,760
to fail any student who leaves early from class

20
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,840
for any reason whatsoever.

21
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,000
So I say, I'm not really saying this,

22
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,320
but imagine anyone who ever leaves early

23
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:19,200
will fail the entire class.

24
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:20,720
That is it.

25
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:21,800
This is the rule.

26
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:23,680
It will be enforced.

27
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:26,040
Choose your course wisely.

28
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:29,320
Now, if you believe my threat, probably none of you

29
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:31,000
will leave early because none of you

30
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,560
will regard whatever it is that you'd

31
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:35,320
be doing instead of staying in class till 10 o'clock

32
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:38,080
to be more important than getting a passing

33
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:39,600
grade in this class.

34
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:42,080
Given that you bother being here at all,

35
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,640
and you've gone through the whole process to get here,

36
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:49,320
it is unlikely that you actually would want to leave,

37
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:50,720
given this threat.

38
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:52,960
Because you'd have to go and throw away all this work

39
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:54,000
that you put in.

40
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,280
And presumably, the reason why you put in all this work

41
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:59,160
is because you anticipate a lot of benefit for yourself,

42
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:01,280
high expected value.

43
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,640
So you do know why you're here.

44
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:06,080
You don't just come.

45
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:09,600
This was not a choice made lightly, I assume.

46
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:10,320
Correct.

47
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:13,760
You didn't just say, well, maybe a PhD in economics

48
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:16,160
might be the order of the day.

49
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,200
Instead, you probably deliberated,

50
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:20,200
and you decided you want to be here.

51
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,400
You don't make a choice like that lightly.

52
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:24,240
Anyway, so if you believe my threat,

53
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:25,760
you're not going to leave early.

54
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:27,480
And therefore, I will never actually

55
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:29,200
have to impose my threat.

56
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:31,000
I won't actually fail anyone if you

57
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,440
believe that I would fail anyone who went and left early.

58
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:37,640
Now, this sounds like a Nash equilibrium.

59
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:43,640
Because if you believe what I said, you won't leave.

60
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:46,320
And since you aren't leaving, there's no incentive for me

61
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:49,280
to go and be reasonable.

62
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:51,000
There's no incentive for me to say, well,

63
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,920
this punishment's awfully harsh, because I never actually

64
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:55,640
impose a punishment.

65
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:57,840
So what's the problem?

66
00:02:57,840 --> 00:02:59,920
It doesn't take any effort on my part

67
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:03,600
to go and have a really harsh punishment that never happens.

68
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:07,520
At least it doesn't seem like it involves a lot of effort.

69
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:12,040
Now, to most people, this seems like an implausible prediction.

70
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:14,320
Sounds like a Nash equilibrium, but it

71
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,840
doesn't sound like a very good Nash equilibrium,

72
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:19,640
because you know I wouldn't want to actually carry out

73
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,520
this threat.

74
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:23,320
So first of all, of course, it might hurt me

75
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:24,320
to carry out the threat.

76
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:28,440
I might feel bad if someone left early because a child was sick.

77
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:30,560
And then I failed you because you

78
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,560
had to go to your sick child.

79
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:35,320
So I might feel bad about doing that.

80
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:38,360
And then given that I might feel bad about doing it,

81
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:41,480
in some cases, maybe I would feel bad

82
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:45,240
if I wasn't sure that your excuse was good.

83
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:47,120
So you might say, I had a really good reason.

84
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,160
And I say, well, I don't know if you had a good reason.

85
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:50,280
And that might weigh on me.

86
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,740
Maybe you had a really good reason.

87
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,360
Maybe I went and failed a person who

88
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:55,040
would have been a fantastic economist,

89
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:58,840
all because of this stupid rule that I enforced so rigidly

90
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:00,920
and with such harsh punishments.

91
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:03,400
And then even if you ignore any of my feelings,

92
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:05,760
maybe I actually enjoy being sadistic.

93
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:08,840
Still, it is likely that any student that

94
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:12,400
was going to fail you in this class, any student that

95
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:16,200
was experiencing the punishment, would complain.

96
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:18,280
Where do you start complaining?

97
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:21,200
I don't do this, but where would you start complaining?

98
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:21,920
The alphabet.

99
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:22,420
What's that?

100
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:22,920
The alphabet.

101
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,040
There's an alphabet today?

102
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:25,360
I don't know.

103
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,240
Maybe.

104
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,840
I assume that you would go to Dan Hauser, the chairman

105
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:31,680
of the Department of Complain, or maybe you

106
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:33,720
go to Chris Coyne, director of graduate studies.

107
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:37,400
So you go to them, and you just say, Kaplan is crazy.

108
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,520
Kaplan is cruel.

109
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:39,240
He is harsh.

110
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:40,160
He's unreasonable.

111
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:41,880
He's a danger to economics.

112
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:43,880
And you go and complain.

113
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:46,200
And maybe you actually convince them.

114
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:48,080
But even if you don't convince them,

115
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:50,240
they might go and email me and say,

116
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:52,040
there's some unhappy students here.

117
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:53,960
They're making my life worse.

118
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:56,680
And then perhaps there could be an implicit threat.

119
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,920
Well, if you do things to people that

120
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:02,880
lead them to make my life bad, who knows what I might do?

121
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:04,680
I might get upset, and then I might

122
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,840
have to go and make somebody else's life bad.

123
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:09,920
Who knows who that would be?

124
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:11,840
But maybe it would be me.

125
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:13,760
So any time that I'm doing something

126
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:16,440
that causes students to do things to other faculty

127
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:18,040
to make their lives bad, and then I

128
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:19,600
have to live with these faculty.

129
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:23,680
And so right now, I'm on track to become the longest serving

130
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:25,720
professor in GMU history.

131
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:28,760
As I started here at 26, I've never had any other job.

132
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:33,320
So I could be here for 70 years with some luck.

133
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:37,040
You know there's no mandatory retirement or anything.

134
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:38,620
Did you include this in your decision

135
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:41,400
to come to get a PhD in economics

136
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:44,120
so that you can go and keep doing this job for as long

137
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:46,720
as you could somehow get to the classroom?

138
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:51,200
And who knows what the system will be in, what is it now?

139
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:52,480
50 years?

140
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:53,680
Who knows what it will be?

141
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:56,240
But anyway, it seems like the gravy train will continue,

142
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:57,000
probably, right?

143
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:57,500
Right?

144
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:00,000
Right.

145
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,080
Came here to seek rents.

146
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:02,240
Yes.

147
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:03,240
Yeah.

148
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:05,120
Yeah, came here to chew gum and seek rents,

149
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:07,760
and I'm all out of gum.

150
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:08,480
All right.

151
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:12,320
So then even if the chairman just rebuffed you and just said

152
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:15,000
whatever Brian says is great, then you

153
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,400
might go and complain to someone higher than the chairman,

154
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,760
complain to the dean, start complaining.

155
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:23,840
It would just be easier just not to go and enforce the rule.

156
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,200
All right.

157
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:27,080
All right.

158
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:29,560
Now, how would a threat that you know that I would never

159
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:32,400
actually carry out actually wind up changing your behavior?

160
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:33,360
It would seem strange.

161
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:36,040
So if you knew that I wouldn't really do it,

162
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:37,440
then it isn't very scary anymore.

163
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:40,000
If it isn't very scary, why should you go and worry

164
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,640
a lot about the threat?

165
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:43,840
You just treat it as an empty threat.

166
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:45,880
You know, like the classic parent's threat

167
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:47,960
on the road to Disneyland to say, look,

168
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:50,500
if you don't start behaving, I'm going to turn this car right

169
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,040
around this minute.

170
00:06:52,040 --> 00:06:54,600
Does anyone ever have a parent who actually carried out

171
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:56,160
a threat like that?

172
00:06:56,160 --> 00:06:56,660
Yes.

173
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:57,200
All right.

174
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:59,040
So there are a few.

175
00:06:59,040 --> 00:06:59,800
Yes.

176
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:02,920
But not very often are these threats carried out.

177
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:03,420
I didn't.

178
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:03,920
I like it.

179
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:05,600
And you're scarred for life after that.

180
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:09,480
So I think some people would be.

181
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:12,080
Yes, once I was on a committee.

182
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:15,720
It was a great appeal committee.

183
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:17,400
So James Buchanan, Nobel Prize winner,

184
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:18,820
was teaching a class, and there was

185
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:22,480
a student who said that he was unfairly graded.

186
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:24,640
Now, do anyone know what the official GMU

187
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:28,720
standard for overturning a professor's grade is?

188
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:31,040
Manifest unfairness.

189
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:32,800
Manifest unfairness.

190
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:35,240
Now, the case was handed to me when I was in my very first

191
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:35,880
year.

192
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:38,280
So right out of graduate school, I

193
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:40,880
was called upon to decide whether a Nobel Prize

194
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:46,360
winner in my own department had been manifestly unfair or not.

195
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:51,080
And in my judgment, it wasn't manifestly unfair.

196
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:54,880
So the student won his grade race from a C to an A,

197
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:57,200
over the word of a Nobel Prize winner.

198
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:59,640
But anyway, so there's a case where

199
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:01,640
the system was very stacked against the student.

200
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:03,800
But if it was an instant fail out of the program,

201
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,440
then who knows.

202
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:08,480
Now, there's a general term in game theory

203
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:11,800
for all problems like this, not just for sub-game perfection.

204
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:14,080
It is called the problem of out of equilibrium play.

205
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:24,080
All right, so because I can optimally

206
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:26,760
choose bizarre behavior in situations

207
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:29,560
that I know will never happen.

208
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:32,880
But then, knowing what I would do in situations that never

209
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:36,840
happen can actually change your behavior in situations

210
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:39,240
that happen all the time.

211
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:41,480
Which when you first hear it, sounds crazy.

212
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:44,840
But this example is pretty clear.

213
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:48,280
If you really believe that someone's extreme threats were

214
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:50,960
true, then it would make sense for you

215
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:52,960
to change your behavioral logs.

216
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,160
Even then, there's a question, but I

217
00:08:55,160 --> 00:08:58,120
know that you wouldn't really do it if we ever

218
00:08:58,120 --> 00:08:59,720
got to that situation.

219
00:08:59,720 --> 00:09:01,160
But in terms of Nash equilibrium,

220
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:02,040
that doesn't matter.

221
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:05,960
Because Nash equilibrium just says that given what I've said,

222
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:07,580
you don't want to change your behavior,

223
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:09,120
given what you've said, you don't want to change your

224
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:09,760
behavior, or given what I'm doing,

225
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:10,560
you don't want to change your behavior,

226
00:09:10,560 --> 00:09:13,020
given what you're doing, I don't want to change my behavior.

227
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:16,400
And it seems like these extreme threats qualify then

228
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:18,360
as Nash equilibrium.

229
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:21,400
And that is odd.

230
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:23,320
So questions about that?

231
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:25,360
See how it's weird?

232
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,640
So you've probably experienced idle threats many times

233
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:29,040
in your life before.

234
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:31,360
And you quickly get a sense that these are idle threats.

235
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:33,200
And normally, they don't change your behavior.

236
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:36,160
Although, if you're very naive or young,

237
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:37,800
you may actually believe the threat

238
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:40,920
to turn the car around on the way to Disneyland.

239
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:41,760
Yes?

240
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:43,920
One example I was just thinking of

241
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:47,920
was at the height of the Cold War, US versus Soviet Union,

242
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:49,520
and mutually assured destruction.

243
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:51,920
I was just wondering if that would be an example of that.

244
00:09:51,920 --> 00:09:52,600
It might be.

245
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:57,840
Although, it's certainly discussed in the same context.

246
00:09:57,840 --> 00:10:00,360
Although, of course, the whole doctrine of mutually assured

247
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:04,440
destruction was, that was one where it wasn't quite that.

248
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:07,640
Are you familiar with Mad Mutually Assured Destruction?

249
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,920
You've seen Dr. Strangelove?

250
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:12,000
All right, so great movie for game theory.

251
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,760
And Dr. Strangelove is actually based very closely

252
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:17,760
on von Neumann, who I've mentioned.

253
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:20,840
But anyway, the idea of mutually assured destruction

254
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:23,280
was you have the US and the Soviet Union.

255
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:27,640
And each believes that if I nuke you, you will nuke me.

256
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:29,800
So I go and use my nuclear weapons on you.

257
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:31,680
You will use your nuclear weapons on me.

258
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:33,320
So no one wants to go and start this,

259
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:34,840
because it will lead to destruction.

260
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:37,520
So it wasn't actually the same thing.

261
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:41,600
It would be more along the lines of,

262
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:45,160
if you use one nuclear weapon, I will unload my full arsenal

263
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:45,840
on you.

264
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:48,560
That one sounds like you wouldn't want to do it, right?

265
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:49,060
Yeah.

266
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:52,040
Because, wait, they do one weapon,

267
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:54,640
which destroys one city, which you like a lot.

268
00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:58,520
But still, then you're going to end the world?

269
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:01,680
So it seems more likely that you would go and then, say,

270
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:04,520
send one missile back at a similar city, or maybe two,

271
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:06,080
just to prove your point.

272
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:08,560
And say, and what are you going to do, launch four?

273
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:10,280
Well, we all know what that's going to lead to.

274
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:11,520
Then I'll have to launch eight, and there

275
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:12,360
will be destruction.

276
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:15,520
So just sit there and let me nuke two of your cities.

277
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:18,720
And then say thank you.

278
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:20,720
Something like that.

279
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:24,760
So anyway, so there's nothing theoretically wrong

280
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:28,480
with the idea that this is a Nash equilibrium for me

281
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:33,200
to make a threat that I wouldn't want to carry out.

282
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:36,080
But there is a refinement of Nash equilibrium,

283
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:38,320
which is called, of course, sub-game perfect Nash

284
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:39,560
equilibrium.

285
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:41,600
Sub-game perfect is a special case.

286
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:43,040
So you could have Nash equilibria

287
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:44,600
that are not sub-game perfect, and they're still

288
00:11:44,600 --> 00:11:46,080
perfectly good Nash equilibrium.

289
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:47,800
They still fit the definition.

290
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,800
Nash equilibria.

291
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,880
But sub-game perfect Nash equilibria

292
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:56,040
are Nash equilibria with an extra property,

293
00:11:56,040 --> 00:11:57,720
with an extra shrink.

294
00:11:57,720 --> 00:11:59,080
All right?

295
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:01,520
What you can think about it is with sub-game perfection

296
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:05,400
requires Nash play in every sub-game of a game.

297
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:06,520
All right?

298
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:08,640
Now, if you want to check for sub-game perfection,

299
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:12,560
step one is you apply backwards induction as far as you can.

300
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:15,840
Remember, we already talked about backwards induction.

301
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:18,920
All right, so if you've got a game of perfect information,

302
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:22,280
then whatever result you get from backwards induction

303
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:24,000
is sub-game perfect.

304
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:24,760
All right?

305
00:12:24,760 --> 00:12:26,320
All right, now a standard example

306
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:28,680
of this, which is often then applied.

307
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:32,040
This is the so-called entry game,

308
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:34,360
which comes out of industrial organization.

309
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:37,320
And here's the idea.

310
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:39,520
All right, so suppose that we have a game where

311
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:42,600
there's an incumbent firm that's already around making profits,

312
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:45,440
and then there's a new firm, the entry, the entrant.

313
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:49,200
All right, now the entrant can either go in or out.

314
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:52,120
And of course, if the entrant goes out,

315
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:54,000
then the entrant is going to make no profits.

316
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,680
And then the firm that remains there

317
00:12:56,680 --> 00:12:59,560
gets to be a monopoly, where it has always been,

318
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:01,800
and gets high profits, say, 10.

319
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:06,320
All right, but if you go in, the same way we're here,

320
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:07,560
the entrant's going first here.

321
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:09,720
The entrant's going first, incumbent's going second.

322
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:12,320
If you go in, that incumbent can do two things.

323
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:17,800
It can either do what we call accommodate,

324
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:20,880
where it goes and shares the market nicely with you.

325
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:24,200
And then maybe you each get, say, profits of three and three,

326
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:25,280
something like that.

327
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:28,600
It's going to be less than the monopoly profits before,

328
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:30,640
because there's competition.

329
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:34,200
So at least there's some kind of competition.

330
00:13:34,200 --> 00:13:36,840
It's no longer going to be the monopoly outcome.

331
00:13:36,840 --> 00:13:39,360
On the other hand, though, it could also

332
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:41,400
do what we call fight.

333
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:44,440
All right, so it could fight.

334
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:48,000
It could say, well, you went and treaded on my territory,

335
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,120
and now it is all out war.

336
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:52,520
And now I'm going to go and give the product away,

337
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:54,400
and we're both going to lose a ton of money.

338
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:57,480
We'll each have, say, a loss of 10.

339
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:01,680
So we'll each get the negative of the monopoly profits.

340
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:07,400
All right, now, if you go and think, so if you're getting,

341
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:10,240
in terms of Nash equilibrium, there's actually two.

342
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:13,760
So one of them is where you do in and accommodate.

343
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:17,320
So the entrant goes in, incumbent accommodates.

344
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:18,720
You both get three, three.

345
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:20,760
And then the entrant doesn't want

346
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:23,640
to switch, because the entrant would go from profits of three

347
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:26,040
to what if he switched?

348
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:28,280
No, he would go from three over.

349
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:31,360
So if the entrant were to switch, he'd get zero.

350
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:33,160
If the entrant switched, then he just

351
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:35,720
wouldn't be in the market at all and get nothing.

352
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:37,360
And then the incumbent doesn't want

353
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:38,780
to switch, because if he switched,

354
00:14:38,780 --> 00:14:40,520
he would go from three to negative 10.

355
00:14:40,520 --> 00:14:44,160
So we have one equilibrium in accommodate.

356
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:45,520
That one's fine.

357
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:47,480
But we also have another one, which

358
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:49,760
is that the entrant stays out.

359
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:53,360
And then the incumbent plays fight if the entrant enters,

360
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:54,840
which he doesn't.

361
00:14:54,840 --> 00:14:57,280
So threatening to both, so fighting

362
00:14:57,280 --> 00:15:01,200
if the entrant doesn't go in costs you nothing.

363
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:04,880
All it's saying is, if you did enter, good thing for you

364
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:05,600
that you didn't.

365
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:07,140
Good thing for both of us you didn't.

366
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:08,560
I would have fought.

367
00:15:08,560 --> 00:15:11,160
And then there would have been a big trade war,

368
00:15:11,160 --> 00:15:12,600
and we both would have lost money.

369
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:15,160
OK.

370
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:15,800
All right.

371
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:17,840
So we have two Nash equilibria.

372
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:23,280
Nash equilibria.

373
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:26,160
So these are both perfectly good Nash equilibria.

374
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:30,280
But only one of them is sub-gay perfect.

375
00:15:30,280 --> 00:15:31,800
Only one of them is sub-gay perfect,

376
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:34,320
and of course it is this one here.

377
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:40,200
Because this one here requires us

378
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:42,280
to believe that the incumbent would do something

379
00:15:42,280 --> 00:15:45,680
in this scenario that is bad for him.

380
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:49,000
So here, this is saying that if you ever did enter,

381
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,160
then I would fight, even though fighting

382
00:15:51,160 --> 00:15:54,440
yields profits of 13 less than accommodating.

383
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:56,520
So again, this is very much like that situation

384
00:15:56,520 --> 00:15:58,880
where I threatened to fail and he student leaves early.

385
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:02,920
Where if you really believed that I would do it,

386
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:04,760
then it would change your behavior.

387
00:16:04,760 --> 00:16:07,080
And then the situation would never come up.

388
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:10,240
But that is a hard to believe scenario.

389
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:11,400
Excuse me, actually.

390
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:13,520
Why the second outcome?

391
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:18,160
And why the new entry form stays out

392
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:20,440
when it's also a Nash equilibrium?

393
00:16:20,440 --> 00:16:21,320
Let's see.

394
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:23,800
So what was I going to say?

395
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:26,120
Also, so here's the thing.

396
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:30,720
So if we're in out fight, then if the entrant were to go in,

397
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:33,520
he would go from getting 0 to getting negative 10.

398
00:16:33,520 --> 00:16:34,720
That's worse for him.

399
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:36,960
And on the other hand, for the incumbent,

400
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:41,040
since he is actually getting 10, if he were to switch over,

401
00:16:41,040 --> 00:16:43,800
if he were to switch over to accommodate,

402
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:44,920
he would still have 10.

403
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:46,560
Because remember, with Nash equilibrium,

404
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:47,680
we're always thinking about what would

405
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:49,360
happen if you change without changing

406
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:51,040
what the other person does.

407
00:16:51,040 --> 00:16:54,000
So right now, we're in out fight.

408
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:55,600
The incumbent is getting 10.

409
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:58,240
And if the incumbent were to go and switch to accommodate,

410
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:00,800
since this is, he's changing his strategy in a situation

411
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:02,600
that isn't even happening, he still gets 10.

412
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:08,240
So you think about this.

413
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:09,560
Actually, this is not a bad way.

414
00:17:09,560 --> 00:17:12,360
So you know how it's very common for militaries

415
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:15,040
to have a big file drawer of contingency plans?

416
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:18,760
This is what we do if England attacks us, things like this.

417
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:20,800
So you know, it's very, you know, so militaries,

418
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:23,200
they're very common to have like a giant file,

419
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:25,080
or now probably there's computers,

420
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:27,200
you know, somewhere a computer.

421
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:29,160
There's a list of all the different weird things

422
00:17:29,160 --> 00:17:30,120
that might happen.

423
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:31,640
And then there's a plan for it.

424
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:33,840
So yeah, so actually, the United States in the 1930s

425
00:17:33,840 --> 00:17:36,880
did have a plan for what if we have to fight England, right?

426
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:39,160
And the idea of sub game perfection

427
00:17:39,160 --> 00:17:41,320
is that if there are plans in those drawers

428
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:43,880
that you would never actually follow through on,

429
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:46,240
then they're not gonna change anyone's behavior.

430
00:17:47,320 --> 00:17:49,480
But Nash equilibrium actually says,

431
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:51,480
well, maybe you go and you write it down.

432
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:56,000
And then if people believe that you would do it,

433
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,680
that would actually change their behavior.

434
00:18:00,520 --> 00:18:01,360
All right.

435
00:18:02,240 --> 00:18:04,200
Now, in games and in-brewing information,

436
00:18:04,200 --> 00:18:06,240
then we can no longer do this,

437
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:07,560
we no longer just do backwards deduction.

438
00:18:07,560 --> 00:18:10,920
Then we have to actually find the Nash equilibrium

439
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:12,560
in each of the sub games.

440
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:15,640
So, as we do more games,

441
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:17,000
we'll actually have more examples of things

442
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:17,840
that you can put at the end,

443
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:19,960
but it wouldn't have to be perfect information.

444
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:22,920
It could also be in any kind of Nash equilibrium

445
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:24,000
would also work at the end,

446
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:29,000
as long as each stage we are doing a Nash strategy.

